1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,560
The wood of a tree is built mostly out of
thin air.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,740
So where does a tree's weight really come
from?

3
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:12,705
By the end of this film, you'll know how a
willow in a pot, a sprig of mint and a

4
00:00:12,745 --> 00:00:17,891
sunny window cracked one of nature's
oldest secrets, and why it took more than

5
00:00:17,931 --> 00:00:19,660
a hundred and fifty years.

6
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:23,880
For two thousand years, people believed
plants ate the soil.

7
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:29,291
The Greek thinker Aristotle taught that
roots take a plant's food from the earth,

8
00:00:29,331 --> 00:00:30,131
like a mouth.

9
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:31,720
It seemed obvious.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:37,806
But if it was wrong, nobody really knew
where wood, or food, or even the air we

11
00:00:37,846 --> 00:00:39,060
breathe came from.

12
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:45,249
Near Brussels, in the first half of the
sixteen-hundreds, a doctor called Jan

13
00:00:45,289 --> 00:00:48,100
Baptist van Helmont decided to test it.

14
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:49,360
He didn't argue.

15
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:50,700
He weighed.

16
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:55,300
He would spend five years watching one
tree in one pot.

17
00:00:55,980 --> 00:01:00,920
What he found broke the old idea, and then
led him straight to the wrong answer.

18
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:05,070
Here is exactly what he did.

19
00:01:05,510 --> 00:01:10,482
He took a big clay pot and filled it with
two hundred pounds of soil, dried in a

20
00:01:10,522 --> 00:01:14,030
furnace first, so no hidden water could
fool the scales.

21
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:18,550
Then he planted a young willow that
weighed five pounds.

22
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:23,916
He covered the pot with a metal lid full
of tiny holes, so rain could get in, but

23
00:01:23,956 --> 00:01:24,850
dust could not.

24
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:30,110
And for five years, he gave that tree
nothing but pure water.

25
00:01:30,490 --> 00:01:32,010
Picture yourself in that garden.

26
00:01:32,730 --> 00:01:34,990
Every spring, the leaves burst out.

27
00:01:35,490 --> 00:01:36,790
Every autumn, they fall.

28
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:42,110
Year after year the willow grows taller,
and the pot of soil never changes.

29
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:47,450
Then, after five years, he pulled the tree
out and weighed it.

30
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:52,010
A hundred and sixty-nine pounds, about the
weight of a grown man.

31
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:56,790
And that didn't even count the leaves that
had fallen in four autumns.

32
00:01:57,050 --> 00:01:58,690
Now came the moment that mattered.

33
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:02,290
He dried the soil again and put it back on
the scales.

34
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:06,370
Two hundred pounds, less about two ounces.

35
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:12,130
A hundred and sixty-four pounds of new
tree, and only two ounces of soil gone.

36
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:14,490
The tree had not eaten the soil.

37
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:16,250
So why had it grown?

38
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:19,130
Van Helmont's answer seemed obvious.

39
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:22,130
The only thing he had added was water.

40
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:27,710
So, he wrote, the wood, the bark and the
roots had come from water alone.

41
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:32,310
It was a brilliant experiment, and his
answer was mostly wrong.

42
00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:35,490
And here is the strangest part of the
mystery.

43
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:41,727
Van Helmont also burned sixty-two pounds
of charcoal and found just one pound of

44
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:42,870
ash left behind.

45
00:02:43,390 --> 00:02:47,970
The rest had vanished into the air, as a
wild, invisible spirit.

46
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:50,910
He gave it a new name: gas.

47
00:02:51,730 --> 00:02:54,630
We now know that gas was mostly carbon
dioxide.

48
00:02:55,190 --> 00:02:58,590
He had discovered the very thing his tree
was made of.

49
00:02:59,130 --> 00:03:00,330
He never put the two together.

50
00:03:03,050 --> 00:03:07,370
Nearly a century later, the question moved
from the soil to the sky.

51
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:13,146
Just outside London, in seventeen twenty-
seven, a country parson called Stephen

52
00:03:13,186 --> 00:03:18,178
Hales published careful measurements of
how much water plants drink up and give

53
00:03:18,218 --> 00:03:19,770
off through their leaves.

54
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:22,090
And he made a bold guess.

55
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:26,850
Plants, he wrote, probably take some of
their food from the air.

56
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:29,370
But a guess isn't proof.

57
00:03:30,170 --> 00:03:31,770
Leeds, seventeen seventy-one.

58
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:37,922
Joseph Priestley, a minister who lived
next door to a brewery, was fascinated by

59
00:03:37,962 --> 00:03:38,762
air.

60
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:44,318
He knew that if you burn a candle inside a
sealed glass jar, the flame soon dies, and

61
00:03:44,358 --> 00:03:47,610
the air left behind won't let another
candle burn.

62
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:50,550
Priestley called that air injured.

63
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:56,166
So, on the seventeenth of August, he
slipped a sprig of mint into a jar of that

64
00:03:56,206 --> 00:03:57,730
injured air, and waited.

65
00:03:58,410 --> 00:04:01,110
Ten days later, he lowered in another
candle.

66
00:04:01,770 --> 00:04:03,190
It burned perfectly well.

67
00:04:03,890 --> 00:04:07,190
Then he tried air that mice had made stale
with their breathing.

68
00:04:07,730 --> 00:04:11,390
In the jar where mint had grown, a mouse
lived perfectly well.

69
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:12,830
Why does that matter?

70
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:18,030
Because every fire and every animal on
Earth uses the air up.

71
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:20,550
Something had to be putting it back.

72
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:26,848
Priestley wrote that the damage animals do
to the air is repaired by "the vegetable

73
00:04:26,888 --> 00:04:29,090
creation." Plants repair the air.

74
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:30,650
But there was a problem.

75
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:33,410
Sometimes his plants repaired the air.

76
00:04:33,870 --> 00:04:35,090
Sometimes they didn't.

77
00:04:35,590 --> 00:04:37,190
And nobody could say why.

78
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:40,120
Summer, seventeen seventy-nine.

79
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:46,579
A Dutch doctor called Jan Ingenhousz took
a house in the countryside just outside

80
00:04:46,619 --> 00:04:52,760
London, and in about three months, he ran
more than five hundred experiments.

81
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:57,420
He plunged fresh leaves into jars of water
and watched.

82
00:04:58,120 --> 00:04:59,540
Picture yourself at his window.

83
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:05,420
In the sunshine you see tiny silver
bubbles appear on the green leaf and rise

84
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:06,260
to the top.

85
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,340
He collected the bubbles and tested the
gas.

86
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:14,040
A candle flame burned brighter in it than
in ordinary air.

87
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:20,167
It was the gas Priestley had found in
seventeen seventy-four, which we now call

88
00:05:20,207 --> 00:05:21,007
oxygen.

89
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:24,020
Then came the clue that solved Priestley's
puzzle.

90
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:28,060
Move the jar into the shade, and the
bubbles stop.

91
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,600
Move it back into the sun, and they start
again.

92
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:34,360
Only the green parts made bubbles.

93
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:36,700
Roots and flowers didn't.

94
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:43,251
And at night, the plant did the opposite:
it made the air slightly worse, just like

95
00:05:43,291 --> 00:05:44,091
an animal.

96
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:47,480
So that was why Priestley's plants
sometimes failed.

97
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:49,120
They needed light.

98
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:50,800
Why light?

99
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:52,760
Because light is energy.

100
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:57,280
A plant catches sunlight and uses it to
make its own food.

101
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:03,440
We now call that photosynthesis, from the
Greek for putting together with light.

102
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:05,377
But one question still remained.

103
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:08,940
The bubbles were something the leaf was
giving out.

104
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:10,980
What was it taking in?

105
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:18,545
In seventeen eighty-two, in Geneva, a
pastor called Jean Senebier showed that

106
00:06:18,585 --> 00:06:23,789
leaves only made their bubbles when the
water held fixed air, the gas we now call

107
00:06:23,829 --> 00:06:24,800
carbon dioxide.

108
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:30,843
Then, in eighteen-oh-four, another
Genevan, Nicolas-Théodore de Saussure, did

109
00:06:30,883 --> 00:06:34,340
what van Helmont had done, but far more
carefully.

110
00:06:34,940 --> 00:06:38,760
He weighed the plant, the water, and the
air around it.

111
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:41,060
And at last, the sums added up.

112
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:45,480
We now know that a leaf takes in carbon
dioxide through tiny pores.

113
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,080
It draws up water through its roots.

114
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:53,611
Then, with the energy of sunlight, it
joins them together into sugar, and lets

115
00:06:53,651 --> 00:06:54,920
the spare oxygen go.

116
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:00,460
That sugar becomes everything else:
leaves, roots, bark and wood.

117
00:07:01,140 --> 00:07:04,520
So here is what van Helmont's willow was
really made of.

118
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:10,494
About half of dry wood is carbon, and
every bit of that carbon came out of the

119
00:07:10,534 --> 00:07:11,334
air.

120
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,760
Most of the rest is oxygen and hydrogen,
from carbon dioxide and water.

121
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:21,240
The soil gave only a pinch of minerals,
which is why his scales barely moved.

122
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:24,200
Van Helmont wasn't completely wrong.

123
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:26,560
Water does go into a tree.

124
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:29,940
But the solid wood is mostly built from
the sky.

125
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:32,080
Why was this so groundbreaking?

126
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:39,012
Because it means almost every bite of food
you have ever eaten began as air and

127
00:07:39,052 --> 00:07:41,080
sunlight, caught by a leaf.

128
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:47,380
Bread, rice, apples, even meat, because
the animal ate plants first.

129
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:50,920
And it explained why the air never runs
out.

130
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:57,136
The oxygen in every breath you take was
given out by plants on land and tiny algae

131
00:07:57,176 --> 00:07:57,976
in the sea.

132
00:07:58,660 --> 00:08:03,470
Today, only about four molecules in every
ten thousand in the air are carbon

133
00:08:03,510 --> 00:08:04,310
dioxide.

134
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:08,540
Out of that thin trace, a willow builds
its wood.

135
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:13,520
A pot of soil, a sprig of mint, and a leaf
in the sun.

136
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:18,220
Together, they proved that a tree isn't
pulled up out of the ground.

137
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:21,120
It's pulled down out of the air.

138
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:25,740
Every one of those carbon atoms is
billions of years old.

139
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:28,940
So what is an atom actually made of?

140
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:34,749
To find out, a young student fired tiny
particles at gold beaten thinner than

141
00:08:34,789 --> 00:08:35,589
paper.
